    era: "Foundational Giants",    bio: "Isaac Newton was born prematurely on Christmas Day 1642 in Woolsthorpe, Lincolnshire, England. His father, a farmer, had died three months before his birth. His mother remarried when Isaac was three, leaving him to be raised by his grandmother \u2014 an abandonment that scarred him deeply and shaped his notoriously prickly personality.\n\nIn 1665, Cambridge University closed due to the Great Plague, and Newton returned home. In that single year of isolation, he developed calculus, began his work on optics, and formulated the law of universal gravitation \u2014 the most productive year any scientist has ever had. His Principia Mathematica (1687) laid out the three laws of motion and the inverse-square law of gravity, explaining everything from falling apples to planetary orbits in a single framework.\n\nNewton also built the first reflecting telescope, proved that white light is composed of colored rays, and spent years as Master of the Royal Mint, where he pursued counterfeiters with unexpected zeal. He was the first scientist to be knighted. His work defined physics for 200 years, until Einstein came along.",
