    era: "Foundational Giants",    image: "/images/scientists/albert-einstein.jpg",
    bio: "Albert Einstein was born in Ulm, Germany. As a child, he was slow to speak \u2014 not talking fluently until age nine \u2014 which led his parents to fear he was developmentally delayed. A compass his father showed him at age five fascinated him: how could an invisible force move the needle? This question would define his life's work.\n\nEinstein was a patent clerk in Bern, Switzerland, when in 1905 \u2014 his own Annus Mirabilis \u2014 he published four papers that changed physics forever: the photoelectric effect (proving light is quantized), Brownian motion (proving atoms exist), special relativity (E=mc\u00b2), and the equivalence of mass and energy. In 1915, he completed general relativity, redefining gravity as the curvature of spacetime.\n\nIn 1933, Einstein fled Nazi Germany and settled at the Institute for Advanced Study in Princeton, New Jersey. He spent his later years searching for a unified field theory \u2014 unsuccessfully \u2014 and became a beloved public figure. His letter to President Roosevelt in 1939 helped initiate the Manhattan Project, though he later advocated for nuclear disarmament. Time magazine named him Person of the Century.",
