    era: "Foundational Giants",    bio: "Galileo Galilei was born in Pisa, Italy. His father wanted him to study medicine, but Galileo was drawn to mathematics. Legend says he dropped objects from the Leaning Tower of Pisa to test Aristotle's theories \u2014 though historians doubt this story, his experiments on falling bodies were revolutionary.\n\nIn 1609, Galileo heard about a Dutch invention that made distant objects appear closer. Without ever seeing one, he built his own telescope \u2014 with 3x magnification, then 8x, then 30x. He turned it to the sky and discovered Jupiter's four largest moons, the phases of Venus, mountains on the Moon, and sunspots. His observations provided concrete proof for Copernicus's heliocentric model.\n\nThe Catholic Church declared heliocentrism heretical. In 1633, Galileo was tried by the Inquisition and forced to recant his views under threat of torture. He spent the rest of his life under house arrest. But his work could not be unwritten. He established the experimental method \u2014 hypothesis, observation, measurement \u2014 that remains the foundation of all scientific inquiry. Stephen Hawking called him 'the father of modern science.'",
