    era: "Foundational Giants",    bio: "Charles Robert Darwin was born in Shrewsbury, England, into a wealthy family. His father was a doctor, his grandfather Erasmus Darwin had proposed an early theory of evolution. Young Charles was an indifferent student who preferred collecting beetles to studying. His father sent him to medical school, but Charles couldn't stand the sight of blood and dropped out.\n\nIn 1831, at age 22, Darwin boarded HMS Beagle for a five-year voyage around the world. In the Gal\u00e1pagos Islands, he observed that finches on different islands had differently shaped beaks \u2014 each adapted to local food sources. This simple observation became the seed of a revolution. By 1838, he had conceived of natural selection, but feared the controversy and waited 20 years to publish.\n\nIn 1859, On the Origin of Species was published and sold out immediately. The idea that humans shared common ancestry with apes scandalized Victorian society. Darwin rarely left his home in later years, suffering from chronic illness, but continued writing. He is buried in Westminster Abbey near Isaac Newton. His theory of evolution by natural selection is the unifying principle of all biology.",
