    era: "Foundational Giants",    image: "/images/scientists/marie-curie.jpg",
    bio: "Maria Sklodowska was born in Warsaw, Poland, then part of the Russian Empire. Her father taught physics; her mother ran a boarding school. Higher education for women was illegal in Russian-controlled Poland, so Maria attended the 'Floating University' \u2014 secret underground classes. In 1891, she moved to Paris to study at the Sorbonne, living in a garret so cold she kept her clothes on in bed.\n\nIn 1895, she married Pierre Curie, and together they began studying uranium rays discovered by Henri Becquerel. They isolated two new elements \u2014 polonium (named after Poland) and radium \u2014 from tons of pitchblende ore in a freezing shed. Marie coined the term 'radioactivity.' In 1903, she became the first woman to win a Nobel Prize (Physics, shared with Pierre and Becquerel). In 1911, she won the Chemistry Prize \u2014 the only person to win Nobel Prizes in two different scientific fields.\n\nCurie's relentless exposure to radiation eventually killed her \u2014 she died of aplastic anemia in 1934. Her laboratory notebooks are still too radioactive to handle. During World War I, she developed mobile X-ray units called 'Little Curies' and drove them to the front lines herself, saving countless soldiers' lives.",
