    era: "Foundational Giants",    bio: "James Clerk Maxwell was born in Edinburgh, Scotland. His mother taught him to read and keep a journal before she died of cancer when he was eight. At Edinburgh Academy, he was nicknamed 'Dafty' for his country accent and homemade clothes. But his mathematical gifts were apparent early \u2014 he published his first scientific paper at age 14.\n\nMaxwell's greatest achievement was unifying electricity, magnetism, and light into a single theoretical framework \u2014 Maxwell's equations. In 1865, he showed that light is an electromagnetic wave and predicted the existence of radio waves, X-rays, and gamma rays \u2014 all before any had been detected. Einstein kept Maxwell's portrait on his study wall and said his work was 'the most profound and the most fruitful that physics has experienced since the time of Newton.'\n\nMaxwell also produced the first color photograph, developed the kinetic theory of gases, and made fundamental contributions to thermodynamics. He died of stomach cancer at 48 \u2014 the same age and disease that had killed his mother. His equations remain the foundation of all modern electrical and communications technology.",
