    era: "Foundational Giants",    bio: "Archimedes was born in Syracuse, a Greek city-state on the island of Sicily. His father was an astronomer, and Archimedes may have studied in Alexandria, Egypt \u2014 the intellectual capital of the ancient world. He then returned to Syracuse, where he spent his life in the service of King Hiero II.\n\nArchimedes' contributions span mathematics, physics, and engineering. He derived the principle of buoyancy \u2014 the famous 'Eureka' moment in his bathtub \u2014 and the law of the lever: 'Give me a place to stand, and I shall move the earth.' He developed a method of exhaustion that foreshadowed integral calculus by nearly 2,000 years. He estimated the value of pi with remarkable accuracy and devised a system for expressing very large numbers.\n\nWhen Roman forces besieged Syracuse in 212 BC, Archimedes designed ingenious war machines \u2014 catapults, the 'claw of Archimedes,' and supposedly mirrors to focus sunlight and set ships on fire. According to legend, a Roman soldier killed him during the sack of the city despite orders to spare him. His last words were allegedly 'Do not disturb my circles.'",
