    era: "Foundational Giants",    bio: "Louis Pasteur was born in Dole, France, the son of a tanner. He was an average student who showed particular talent for art \u2014 his pastel portraits of family members are still displayed. He studied at the \u00c9cole Normale Sup\u00e9rieure in Paris and made his first discovery in crystallography: tartrate crystals exist in mirror-image forms, founding the field of stereochemistry.\n\nPasteur's most important work came from his disproof of spontaneous generation \u2014 the idea that life arises from non-life. He showed that microorganisms cause fermentation and spoilage, leading to the process of pasteurization. He extended this to medicine, developing the germ theory of disease. He created the first vaccines for rabies and anthrax, saving countless human and animal lives.\n\nIn 1888, the Pasteur Institute was established in Paris with Pasteur as director \u2014 it remains one of the world's leading biomedical research centers. Pasteur suffered a stroke in his 40s that left him partially paralyzed, but he continued working until his death. He is universally regarded as one of history's greatest benefactors of humanity.",
